Compose an **epic boss-battle theme**, a short cinematic combat cue for a
towering final-boss encounter, using only the sequencer tool.

The cue must capture that identity: a **dark, driving, menacing** orchestral
piece — a relentless low percussive pulse under aggressive low-brass stabs and
tense tremolo strings, a biting ostinato and ominous choir swells, high-stakes
and propulsive, charged with dread and adrenaline. It is about **30 seconds**
long and should loop cleanly so it can play for the length of a sustained fight.
You sequence it note by note as instrument tracks over the `cinematic` instrument
bank.

## How the tool works

The only way to make sound is the `music` binary already on your `PATH`. It is the
sole channel: you build the cue by calling it **one operation at a time**, and the
ordered list of operations you issue — recorded to `actions.json` — is the
**authoritative output that is scored**, not any file you write by hand. Anything
produced another way is discarded and flagged.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags: set the tempo and meter,
`define-track` an instrument from the `cinematic` bank, `add-note` the events, and
`set-track-fx` for gain, pan, and reverb. Unlike a drawing tool, `music` does
**not** re-render after every call — rendering is a separate step. Run
**`music render`** to mix the cue to `music.wav`, draw the waveform + spectrogram +
**piano-roll** preview at `{{workspace}}`, and emit the portable `music.mid` score.
You cannot hear the clip, so **read the preview after you render** to judge your
progress. A config, an empty log, and the `cinematic` bank are already in place at
`{{workspace}}`.

Run `music --help` to list every operation and `music <operation> --help` for one
operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. Inspect the
instruments the `cinematic` bank offers before naming them on your tracks; which
voices you use is your choice.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Sequence the cue up operation by operation — tempo and meter, the tracks and their
notes, the mix — rendering to check the piano-roll and waveform as you go, until it
captures the theme's identity: a dark, driving, menacing boss-battle cue, tense and
propulsive and high-stakes, stereo, within the 30000 ms cap and loopable. When you
are satisfied, stop: the recorded `actions.json` is your finished submission.
